  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Data exploration.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` view covers about 12,800 requests with 200 responses, 800 with 404 and 440 with 503. The 5xx traffic is therefore all `503`. The top countries by volume are CN, IN and US. Timestamps span 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-e448b00e Web logs overview`** (ID `eff-e448b00e-web-logs-overview-6216ce75fb78`) has six panels:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Unique client IPs (metric)\n- Total bytes served (metric)\n- Requests over time (line, by date histogram)\n- Responses by status code (pie)\n- Top countries by requests (bar)\n\nI set the dashboard time range to 2026-10-01 through 2027-01-01. The default last-7-days window would have shown almost nothing, since today is 2026-10-12 and the data runs to December.\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-e448b00e 5xx spike`** (ID `519aaf79-7f9f-48f0-a061-5e5495a25621`) is an Elasticsearch query rule:\n- Runs every 1 minute.\n- Counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]`, so it covers every 5xx code, not only 503.\n- Fires when the count in a 5-minute window is greater than 10.\n- Enabled, with no connector actions attached. The task didn't ask for a notification, so it will only show in the Kibana alerts list.",
